            "text": "pytest.ini sets `testpaths = lib`, but since we explicitly pass `oracle_tests`, that gets overridden. `python_files = test_*.py` applies — our test files need to be named test_*.py. Good.\n\nAlso, the root `pytest.ini` applies when running from the repo root (rootdir=/testbed). `lib/matplotlib/testing/conftest.py`'s `pytest_configure` sets filterwarnings and such — but that conftest only applies to tests under lib/matplotlib, not to oracle_tests. Wait — `pytest_configure` inside a conftest.py is a hook that applies globally if the conftest is loaded. conftest.py files are loaded for the rootdir and the test paths being collected. oracle_tests is not under lib/matplotlib, so that conftest won't be loaded. Good — the environment will be raw.\n\nImportant: without matplotlib's `mpl_test_settings` fixture, rcParams might not be reset between tests. My conftest handles that.\n\nAlso, default warning filters: pytest displays warnings but doesn't error on them. No problem.\n\nNow, is matplotlib importable as a development build? Yes (confirmed). numpy is available.\n\nAlright, let me write the conftest and test files.\n\nTest list (final version):\n\n```python\n# oracle_tests/conftest.py\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"agg\")\n\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef _restore_rcparams():\n    orig = dict(matplotlib.rcParams)\n    yield\n    matplotlib.rcParams.update(orig)\n\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef _close_all_figures():\n    yield\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n    plt.close(\"all\")\n```\n\nTest file `oracle_tests/test_axes_base_clear.py`:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nUnit tests for `_AxesBase.__clear` (lib/matplotlib/axes/_base.py).\n\n`__clear` is the private implementation behind `Axes.clear()` / `Axes.cla()`.\nSince the name is mangled, it can be accessed as `_AxesBase__clear`.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pytest\n\nimport matplotlib as mpl\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom matplotlib import cbook\nfrom matplotlib.axes import Axes\nfrom matplotlib.lines import Line2D\nfrom matplotlib.text import Text\nfrom matplotlib.ticker import AutoMinorLocator, NullLocator\n\n\ndef _new_axes(**kwargs):\n    fig = plt.figure()\n    return fig.add_subplot(**kwargs)\n```\n\nTests:\n\n1. test_clear_method_exists_and_is_used_by_clear\n```python\ndef test_clear_is_private_implementation():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    assert hasattr(ax, \"_AxesBase__clear\")\n    assert not ax._subclass_uses_cla\n    calls = []\n    original = ax._AxesBase__clear\n    ax._AxesBase__clear = lambda: (calls.append(\"clear\"), original())[1]\n    ax.clear()\n    ax.cla()\n    assert calls == [\"clear\", \"clear\"]  # both dispatch to __clear\n```\nWait — `original()` returns None. Tuple index [1] = None. calls gets appended to twice. Good. But careful: `ax._AxesBase__clear = ...` sets an instance attribute. `self.__clear()` inside `clear()` → `self._AxesBase__clear` → instance attribute → shadowing works (verified).\n\n2. test_clear_removes_artists\n```python\ndef test_clear_removes_artists():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3])\n    ax.scatter([1, 2], [2, 1])\n    ax.imshow(np.random.rand(3, 3))\n    ax.text(0.5, 0.5, \"hello\")\n    ax.fill_between([0, 1], [0, 1])\n    assert ax.lines and ax.collections and ax.images and ax.texts and ax.patches\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax._children == []\n    assert len(ax.lines) == 0\n    assert len(ax.collections) == 0\n    assert len(ax.images) == 0\n    assert len(ax.texts) == 0\n    assert len(ax.patches) == 0\n    assert len(ax.tables) == 0\n    # Only structural children remain (spines, axes, title, background patch)\n    children = ax.get_children()\n    assert ax.title in children\n    assert ax.patch in children\n    assert ax.xaxis in children\n```\n\nWait — ax.texts: does ax.text() add to texts? Yes. But note that `ax.title` and such are Text but not included in `ax.texts` (they're stored separately in `_children`). get_children includes title and patch, etc.\n\n3. test_clear_resets_view_limits_and_autoscale\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_view_limits_and_autoscale():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([10, 20], [30, 40])\n    ax.set_xlim(-5, 100)\n    ax.set_ylim(-5, 100)\n    ax.set_autoscale_on(False)\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax.get_xlim() == (0, 1)\n    assert ax.get_ylim() == (0, 1)\n    assert ax.get_autoscale_on()\n    assert ax.get_autoscalex_on()\n    assert ax.get_autoscaley_on()\n```\n\n4. test_clear_resets_scales\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_scales_to_linear():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.set_xscale(\"log\")\n    ax.set_yscale(\"symlog\")\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.get_xscale() == \"linear\"\n    assert ax.get_yscale() == \"linear\"\n```\n\n5. test_clear_ignores_existing_data_limits\n```python\ndef test_clear_ignores_stale_data_limits():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([100, 200], [300, 400])\n    ax.figure.canvas.draw()  # finalize dataLim\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.ignore_existing_data_limits is True\n    ax.plot([0, 1], [0, 1])\n    assert ax.get_xlim() == pytest.approx((-0.05, 1.05))\n    assert ax.get_ylim() == pytest.approx((-0.05, 1.05))\n```\nDefault margin 0.05 → xlim (-0.05, 1.05). Verified with a similar case earlier (9.5, 20.5).\n\n6. test_clear_resets_title_and_labels\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_title_and_labels():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.set_title(\"center\")\n    ax.set_title(\"left\", loc=\"left\")\n    ax.set_title(\"right\", loc=\"right\")\n    ax.set_xlabel(\"xlab\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"ylab\")\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax.get_title() == \"\"\n    assert ax.get_title(loc=\"left\") == \"\"\n    assert ax.get_title(loc=\"right\") == \"\"\n    assert ax.get_xlabel() == \"\"\n    assert ax.get_ylabel() == \"\"\n    assert ax.xaxis.label.get_text() == \"\"\n```\n\n7. test_clear_recreates_title_objects\n```python\ndef test_clear_recreates_title_objects():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    old_title, old_left, old_right = ax.title, ax._left_title, ax._right_title\n    ax.set_title(\"t\")\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.title is not old_title\n    assert ax._left_title is not old_left\n    assert ax._right_title is not old_right\n    assert ax.title.get_position() == (0.5, 1.0)\n    assert ax._left_title.get_position() == (0.0, 1.0)\n    assert ax._right_title.get_position() == (1.0, 1.0)\n    assert ax.title.get_horizontalalignment() == \"center\"\n    assert ax._left_title.get_horizontalalignment() == \"left\"\n    assert ax._right_title.get_horizontalalignment() == \"right\"\n    for t in (ax.title, ax._left_title, ax._right_title):\n        assert t.get_verticalalignment() == \"baseline\"\n        assert t.axes is ax\n        assert t.figure is ax.figure\n```\n\nWait — `_set_artist_props` sets `a.axes = self; a.figure = self.figure`. Let me check _set_artist_props: yes, it sets artist.axes and figure. Verified? Not yet, but standard. Also the position when titley is default is None → y=1.0.\n\n8. test_clear_resets_legend_containers_current_image\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_legend_containers_and_current_image():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([1, 2], [1, 2], label=\"line\")\n    ax.legend()\n    ax.bar([0, 1], [1, 2])\n    im = ax.imshow(np.random.rand(3, 3))\n    ax._sci(im)\n    assert ax.legend_ is not None\n    assert ax.containers\n    assert ax._gci() is im\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax.legend_ is None\n    assert ax.containers == []\n    assert ax._gci() is None\n    assert ax.get_legend() is None\n```\n\n9. test_clear_resets_callback_registry\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_callback_registry():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    old_callbacks = ax.callbacks\n    calls = []\n    ax.callbacks.connect(\"xlim_changed\", lambda a: calls.append(a))\n    ax.callbacks.connect(\"ylim_changed\", lambda a: calls.append(a))\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert isinstance(ax.callbacks, cbook.CallbackRegistry)\n    assert ax.callbacks is not old_callbacks\n    # old connections have vanished\n    ax.set_xlim(0, 5)\n    ax.set_ylim(0, 5)\n    assert calls == []\n    # the new registry declares the expected signals\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"zlim_changed\"):\n        ax.callbacks.process(\"not_a_signal\", ax)\n    ax.callbacks.process(\"zlim_changed\", ax)  # valid signal, no listeners\n```\n\nHmm — does `set_xlim` trigger the xlim_changed callback? set_xlim fires the \"xlim_changed\" callback via the axis... `_set_lim` fires `self.axes.callbacks.process(f'{axis_name}lim_changed', self.axes)`. After clear with listeners disconnected — calls remains empty. But wait: during clear, `_set_lim(0, 1, auto=True)` is called; if the old listener were still connected, it would have fired during clear too. No problem.\n\nAlso `ax.callbacks.process(\"zlim_changed\", ax)` — valid signal with no listeners → no error.\n\n10. test_clear_regenerates_patch\n```python\ndef test_clear_regenerates_patch():\n    ax = _new_axes(facecolor=\"red\")\n    old_patch = ax.patch\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.patch is not old_patch\n    assert mcolors.same_color(ax.patch.get_facecolor(), \"red\")  # from ax._facecolor\n    assert ax.patch.get_facecolor() == mcolors.to_rgba(ax._facecolor)\n    assert ax.patch.get_edgecolor() == mcolors.to_rgba(\"none\")\n    assert ax.patch.get_linewidth() == 0\n    assert ax.patch.figure is ax.figure\n    np.testing.assert_allclose(ax.patch.get_transform().get_matrix(), ax.transAxes.get_matrix())\n```\n\nHmm, `to_rgba('none')` returns (0,0,0,0). The patch's edgecolor was 'none' → rgba (0,0,0,0). In my verification it showed as `(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)`. Good.\n\nAlso for default facecolor: `_facecolor` is rcParams's axes.facecolor 'white'.\n\n11. test_clear_patch_visibility: non-shared resets to visible; shared preserves.\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_patch_visibility_when_unshared():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.patch.set_visible(False)\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.patch.get_visible()  # new patch, default visible\n\ndef test_clear_preserves_visibility_for_shared_x():\n    fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, sharex=True)\n    ax2.xaxis.set_visible(False)\n    ax2.patch.set_visible(False)\n    ax2.clear()\n    assert not ax2.xaxis.get_visible()\n    assert not ax2.patch.get_visible()\n    # master axes unaffected\n    assert ax1.xaxis.get_visible()\n    assert ax1.patch.get_visible()\n\ndef test_clear_preserves_visibility_for_shared_y():\n    fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n    ax2.yaxis.set_visible(False)\n    ax2.patch.set_visible(False)\n    ax2.clear()\n    assert not ax2.yaxis.get_visible()\n    assert not ax2.patch.get_visible()\n```\n\nWait — in sharey, `_sharey is not None` → yaxis visible is restored, patch visible is also restored. Also the xaxis in sharey: not shared → xaxis visible stays as-is (wasn't hidden). Good.\n\nAlso verify that visible=True is preserved in shared axes (stash True and restore True): if ax2 is shared and both are visible → stays visible after clear.\n\n12. test_clear_axis_on\n```python\ndef test_clear_turns_axes_on():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.set_axis_off()\n    assert not ax.axison\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.axison\n```\n\n13. test_clear_misc_state\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_misc_state():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ln, = ax.plot([1, 2], [1, 2])\n    ln.set_mouseover(True)\n    ax.mouseover... # no, use _mouseover_set\n    ax._tight = True\n    ax._use_sticky_edges = False\n    ax.child_axes.append(\"junk\")\n    ax._projection_init = (\"something\", {})\n    old_mouseover = ax._mouseover_set\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax.ignore_existing_data_limits is True\n    assert ax._tight is None\n    assert ax._use_sticky_edges is True\n    assert ax.child_axes == []\n    assert ax._projection_init is None\n    assert ax._mouseover_set is not old_mouseover\n    assert len(ax._mouseover_set) == 0\n    assert ax.stale is True\n```\n\nNote on `ax.stale` — clear sets stale=True at the end. But does `ax.clear()` trigger a subsequent draw? No. But note: does reading `ax.stale` reset it? The `Artist.stale` property returns `_stale` without resetting. Good.\n\nWait — `ax.mouseover`: the `ax.mouseover` property (deprecated bool?) existed — avoid using it. Use `ln.set_mouseover(True)`, which adds it to `_mouseover_set`.\n\n14. test_clear_margins\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_margins_to_rcdefaults():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.margins(0.4, 0.6)\n    assert ax._xmargin == 0.4 and ax._ymargin == 0.6\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax._xmargin == mpl.rcParams[\"axes.xmargin\"]\n    assert ax._ymargin == mpl.rcParams[\"axes.ymargin\"]\n\ndef test_clear_reads_margins_from_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"axes.xmargin\": 0.1, \"axes.ymargin\": 0.2}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.margins(0.5, 0.5)\n        ax.clear()\n        assert ax._xmargin == 0.1\n        assert ax._ymargin == 0.2\n```\n\nHmm — rc_context exits before the assertions... clear happens inside the context. No problem.\n\n15. test_clear_grid_rcparams\n```python\ndef test_clear_turns_grid_off_by_default():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.grid(True)\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax._gridOn is False  # == rcParams['axes.grid']\n    assert not ax.xaxis._major_tick_kw[\"gridOn\"]\n    assert not ax.yaxis._minor_tick_kw[\"gridOn\"]\n\ndef test_clear_applies_grid_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"axes.grid\": True, \"axes.grid.which\": \"major\", \"axes.grid.axis\": \"x\"}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.clear()\n        assert ax._gridOn is True\n        assert ax.xaxis._major_tick_kw[\"gridOn\"] is True? \n```\n\nHmm, `self.grid(self._gridOn, which=..., axis=...)` — Axes.grid → only xaxis.grid(True, which='major'). Axis.grid sets `self._major_tick_kw['gridOn'] = visible`... let me check the Axis.grid implementation to see what it sets. Also, the assignment of `_gridOn`: `self._gridOn = mpl.rcParams['axes.grid']` → True.\n\nRegarding grid axis='x' which='major': xaxis._major_tick_kw['gridOn'] is True; xaxis._minor_tick_kw['gridOn'] — Axis.clear set it to `rc['axes.grid'] and which in ('both','minor')` → False; then grid(False) is called on both axes (all of them), then grid(True, which='major', axis='x'). So xaxis minor is False, yaxis is False.\n\nLet me check Axis.grid:",
