            "text": "OK, Axis.grid uses `set_tick_params(which=..., gridOn=visible)` to update `_major_tick_kw`/`_minor_tick_kw` and tick. Good enough — the tests will assert on `_major_tick_kw['gridOn']` / `_minor_tick_kw['gridOn']` and, depending on the case, on the visibility of the tick gridlines.\n\n16. test_clear_minor_locator_rcparams\n```python\ndef test_clear_minor_locators_default():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.xaxis.set_minor_locator(AutoMinorLocator())\n    ax.clear()\n    assert isinstance(ax.xaxis.get_minor_locator(), NullLocator)\n    assert isinstance(ax.yaxis.get_minor_locator(), NullLocator)\n\ndef test_clear_minor_locators_from_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"xtick.minor.visible\": True, \"ytick.minor.visible\": False}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.clear()\n        assert isinstance(ax.xaxis.get_minor_locator(), AutoMinorLocator)\n        assert isinstance(ax.yaxis.get_minor_locator(), NullLocator)\n```\n\nWait — after axis.clear(), what will the default minor locator be? Axis.clear → _init() → does it set the locator? XAxis._init: `self.set_major_locator(AutoLocator()); self.set_minor_locator(NullLocator())`. I think so. I've confirmed that after clear with default rc, `minor locator: NullLocator` — yes.\n\n17. test_clear_prop_cycle\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_property_cycle():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    old_get_lines = ax._get_lines\n    old_get_patches = ax._get_patches_for_fill\n    (l1,) = ax.plot([1], [1])\n    (l2,) = ax.plot([2], [2])\n    assert l1.get_color() != l2.get_color()\n\n    ax.clear()\n\n    assert ax._get_lines is not old_get_lines\n    assert ax._get_patches_for_fill is not old_get_patches\n    (l3,) = ax.plot([3], [3])\n    assert l3.get_color() == l1.get_color()\n```\n\n18. test_clear_spines\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_spines():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.spines[\"left\"].set_position((\"outward\", 10))\n    ax.spines[\"bottom\"].set_bounds(0, 2)?? \n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.spines[\"left\"].get_position() == (\"outward\", 0)  # hmm\n```\n\nWait — get_position returns `self._position`, which after clear becomes `('outward', 0.0)`. Actually I've confirmed that after clear `_position == ('outward', 0.0)`. But wait — does set_position save `('outward', 10)`? `spine.set_position(('outward', 10))` saves `self._position = ('outward', 10)`. After clear → spine.clear() sets `_position=None`, and then something (maybe axis.clear → reset_ticks → set_clip_path... no, not that) re-secures the position to the default ('outward', 0.0). Actually, during `__clear` after the spine clear: `axis.clear()` for each axis; `spine.register_axis`? Hmm. In my experiment I read `_position` directly and got ('outward', 0.0). Maybe because `self.grid(False)` → Axis.grid → set_tick_params → ... no. `_set_lim` → autoscale → viewLim → spine._adjust_location? That happens at draw time.\n\nHmm, actually `get_position` secures it. But I output `ax.spines['left']._position` directly. Oh well — the observed result is deterministic: after clear, `_position == ('outward', 0.0)`. But to be safe, I'll call `get_position()` (which secures the default) and assert `== ('outward', 0.0)` — this passes whether `_position` was None or already re-secured.\n\nAlso assert that the spines mapping still has the standard keys: left/right/top/bottom.\n\nAlso, spine's axis link: `ax.spines['left'].axis is ax.yaxis`.\n\n19. test_clear_units_and_converter\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_units_and_converter():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], [1, 2, 3])\n    assert ax.xaxis.converter is not None\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.xaxis.converter is None\n    assert ax.xaxis.units is None\n```\n\nHmm — when plotting strings: the converter gets set at autoscale/draw time? `ax.plot(['a','b','c'], ...)` → `_process_plot_var_args` → `convert_xunits`. Is the converter set immediately at plot time? Units are attached in `_add_...`? Actually `Line2D` data goes through `ax._process_unit_info([(\"x\", xdata), (\"y\", ydata)])` at plot time → this sets the converter. Let me quickly verify with a validation script.\n\n20. test_clear_titley_rcparam\n```python\ndef test_clear_titley_default():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax._autotitlepos is True\n    assert ax.title.get_position() == (0.5, 1.0)\n\ndef test_clear_titley_from_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"axes.titley\": 0.5}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.clear()\n        assert ax._autotitlepos is False\n        assert ax.title.get_position() == (0.5, 0.5)\n        assert ax._left_title.get_position() == (0.0, 0.5)\n        assert ax._right_title.get_position() == (1.0, 0.5)\n```\n\n21. test_clear_title_font_rcparams\n```python\ndef test_clear_title_fontproperties_from_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"axes.titlesize\": 22, \"axes.titleweight\": \"bold\"}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.clear()\n        for t in (ax.title, ax._left_title, ax._right_title):\n            assert t.get_size() == 22\n            assert t.get_weight() == \"bold\"\n\ndef test_clear_titlepad_from_rcparams():\n    with mpl.rc_context({\"axes.titlepad\": 12.0}):\n        ax = _new_axes()\n        ax.clear()\n        assert ax.titleOffsetTrans._t == (0.0, 12.0 / 72)\n```\n\nHmm — titleOffsetTrans is also created in `__init__`; clear recreates it with rc's titlepad. Note: `set_title(pad=...)` also changes it, but we're testing the clear path. Note: `axes.titlepad` might accept None → defaults to 6.0? rc's titlepad default is 6.0. Setting set_title with pad and then clearing → resets to rc value. Let me add that:\n\n```python\ndef test_clear_resets_title_offset():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.set_title(\"T\", pad=50)\n    assert ax.titleOffsetTrans._t == (0.0, 50/72)\n    ax.clear()\n    assert ax.titleOffsetTrans._t == (0.0, mpl.rcParams[\"axes.titlepad\"]/72)\n```\n\nWait, does set_title(pad=50) set `_set_title_offset_trans(50)`? Let me check set_title... yes, it sets titleOffsetTrans with pad. But careful: the default rc titlepad is 6.0. After set_title(pad=50), `_t` is (0, 50/72). After clear → (0, 6/72). Good.\n\n22. test_clear_shared_axes_resyncs_limits_and_scale\n```python\ndef test_clear_shared_x_resyncs_from_master():\n    fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, sharex=True)\n    ax1.set_xscale(\"log\")\n    ax1.set_xlim(1, 100)\n    ax2.clear()\n    assert ax2.get_xscale() == \"log\"\n    assert ax2.get_xlim() == (1, 100)\n    assert ax2._sharex is ax1\n    # still joined in the shared grouper\n    assert ax1 in ax2._shared_axes[\"x\"].get_siblings(ax2)\n\ndef test_clear_shared_y_resyncs_from_master():\n    fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n    ax1.set_ylim(-3, 7)\n    ax2.set_ylim(0, 1)  # will be overwritten by sharing anyway\n    ax2.clear()\n    assert ax2.get_ylim() == (-3, 7)\n    assert ax2._sharey is ax1\n```\n\nHmm, wait: with sharey, setting ax1.set_ylim causes ax2's ylim to be synced too. ax2.clear() → calls sharey(ax1) again → set_ylim from ax1. Verified earlier: (0,10) is synced.\n\nNote: `ax1.set_xscale('log')` on a shared axis — does scale propagate to ax2? In sharex, major locator/formatter are shared (xaxis.major is the same object). Scale: `sharex()` copies `other.xaxis._scale`. Verified earlier: after ax1.set_xscale('log'), ax2.clear() → ax2.get_xscale() == 'log'. Yes, from verify3 (that script errored before reaching it, hmm — actually verify3 errored before output on projection; the shared scale check in verify_clear2 didn't run either (error before that). The last section of verify_clear2 didn't run due to an exception. Let me re-verify the shared scale behavior and units behavior before finalizing.\n\n23. test_clear_plain_axes_cla_alias\n```python\ndef test_cla_and_clear_are_interchangeable_on_plain_axes():\n    ax = _new_axes()\n    ax.plot([1, 2], [3, 4])\n    ax.cla()\n    assert len(ax.lines) == 0\n    ax.plot([1, 2], [3, 4])\n    ax.clear()\n    assert len(ax.lines) == 0\n```\n\n24. test_subclass_cla_dispatch\n```python\ndef test_subclass_overriding_cla_dispatch():\n    with pytest.warns(mpl.MatplotlibDeprecationWarning, match=\"Overriding `Axes.cla`\"):\n        class LegacyAxes(Axes):\n            name = \"legacytest\"\n            cla_called = 0\n            def cla(self):\n                super().cla()\n                type(self).cla_called += 1  # hmm, better to use an instance attribute\n    assert LegacyAxes._subclass_uses_cla\n    \n    fig = plt.figure()\n    ax = LegacyAxes(fig, [0, 0, 1, 1])\n    fig.add_axes(ax)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm — does `mpl.MatplotlibDeprecationWarning` exist? `matplotlib.MatplotlibDeprecationWarning` — yes, exported in `__init__`. warn_external is called with category=MatplotlibDeprecationWarning, but the warning instance's class is `PendingDeprecationWarning`-based? Let me look at `_generate_deprecation_warning`: it creates a class via `type(name, (PendingDeprecationWarning,...)`? Let me check:\n\n```python\ndef _generate_deprecation_warning(since, message='', name='', alternative='', pending=False, obj_type='attribute', addendum='', *, removal=''):\n    ...\n    if pending:\n        category = PendingDeprecationWarning\n    else:\n        category = DeprecationWarning\n    warning = type(...)(message % {...})\n```\n\nHmm, and then `warn_external(warning, category=MatplotlibDeprecationWarning)`. warn_external: if warning has no category...? Actually `warnings.warn(warning, category)` — if warning is an instance, category is ignored unless the instance's class is exactly Warning. Let me read _generate_deprecation_warning and warn_external in full to determine what to catch. In verify1, the catch_warnings recorded a warning whose message matched 'Overriding `Axes.cla`'. Let me inspect that class.\n",
